From: rehanraza786 <rehanraza786@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 02:38:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] Anchor dismissals to the repo that owns the gap, or
 refuse
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The dismissal branch was the one place still writing to the multi-root
workspace parent, with a warning on stderr. A note is easy to miss and the
result is an unversioned file — the exact bug the surrounding commit exists
to fix.

A dismissal has no evidence FILE, but it does have evidence: the thing being
dismissed. An orphan topic has producers, a drift table has access sites, an
unresolved expression carries a path, and every path is repo-prefixed — so
the graph can say which repo owns the argument. Resolved through msg_edges,
db_access, db_defs and the http tables.

One owner, write there. Several owners or none, refuse and name the
candidates, which is what save_decision already does for an unanchored
multi-root ADR. That leaves one rule for the whole system: anchor to the
evidence; if the evidence is ambiguous, refuse and say what to pass.

run_tests.py gains both halves — a dismissal lands in a git-versioned repo,
and an untraceable one is refused rather than written to the parent — plus
commit_all_repos(), because anchoring writes into repos legitimately dirties
those worktrees until committed, which the freshness assertions measure.
